  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1055 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nDense fog will once again be the main concern overnight with the \nHays area having the highest probability of occurrence. Short range \nmodels show this fog advecting southeastward towards sunrise and may \naffect areas east of Highway 283. Any fog that does form looks to \ndissipate by late morning. With the exception of a few high clouds, \nmostly clear skies are expected through tomorrow. Winds will be \nlight and from a southerly direction through tonight then shift to a \nnortherly direction tomorrow. As for temperatures, lows this morning \nwill dip into the mid to upper 30s with highs this afternoon in the \nlow to mid 60s. Lows tonight will dip into the upper 30s with highs \ntomorrow in the mid to upper 60s. \n\nThe remainder of the forecast looks dry as an upper level ridge \nbuilds over the Plains through early next week with high pressure \nbeing the dominate feature at the surface. Mid range models show a \ncold front move through the area mid week. At this time the NBM \ndepicts no increase in precipitation as this boundary moves through. \nHighs through this period will generally be in the low 60s to low \n70s with lows in the upper 30s to 40s.",
